WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.550
- Just three episodes of the dystopian

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:06.240
fan-favorite series "Black Mirror"

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.960
have landed on Netflix.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:08.793
As with other seasons,

00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:11.390
showrunners Charlie
Brooker and Annabel Jones

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.840
have included some Easter eggs

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.060
and nods to previous episodes.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.730
We've taken a forensic
deep dive to show you

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.320
34 things that you might have missed

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.293
in season five of "Black Mirror."

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.220
Warning: If you haven't
seen the latest season,

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.033
you might want to look away now.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.020
Episode one, "Striking Vipers,"

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.070
stars "Avengers" actor Anthony Mackie

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.310
as a man romantically linked to a VR game.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.570
The game Mackie's
character, Danny, is gifted

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.970
is made by TCKR.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.400
You might recognize the
neural-research company

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.860
from the season four
episode "Black Museum."

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.960
It developed the pain-diagnostic service

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.160
used by Dr. Peter Dawson.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.730
The company also developed the
alternative-reality simulator

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.210
in the beloved episode "San Junipero."

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.100
The acronym TCKR is also referenced

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.653
in the bar named Tucker's in that episode.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.270
The idea of augmented-reality gaming

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.440
isn't new to "Black Mirror."

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.150
It's the premise for "Playtest,"

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.000
the episode in which TCKR Systems

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.660
is also referenced briefly
in the magazine headline.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:17.760
Think the production design

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.140
and costumes look a bit familiar?

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.160
The pastel palette bears
an uncanny resemblance

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.523
to the community in
season three's "Nosedive."

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.590
In order to access the
game, Danny and Cole

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:33.490
have to put small round
devices onto their temples.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.240
Similar technology is also seen

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.530
throughout the seasons,

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.780
as a mind-reading device in "Crocodile"

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.980
or to extract consciousness
in "Black Museum."

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.500
When Danny opens his cupboard
to put the game away,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.690
we can visibly see an out-of-place-looking

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.260
blue convertible model on the shelf.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.860
Retro cars make an appearance in

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.610
several "Black Mirror" episodes

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.777
such as "Black Museum" and "San Junipero."

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:01.800
It's episode two, "Smithereens,"

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.633
where we start to see tons of references.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.840
It stars "Sherlock" actor Andrew Scott

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.060
as a disillusioned rideshare driver

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.540
who takes an intern from an

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.600
internet-privacy company hostage.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.320
The episode makes no secret
of Brooker and Jones' attitude

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.350
towards smartphone addiction.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.230
The payoff about Scott causing
death by dangerous driving

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.750
because he was checking his phone

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.330
is a recognizable theme about

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.220
the dangers of app usage.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.053
We've seen it in

00:02:29.053 --> 00:02:30.893
"Nosedive" and "Shut Up and Dance."

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.630
You might not recognize
the name of the episode

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.650
unless if you got to
one of the many endings

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:38.360
of "Bandersnatch"

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.290
in which UKN news footage of Stefan's game

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.200
is played with a crawl across
the bottom of the screen.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.330
This reads "Senate committee grills

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:49.553
Smithereen CEO Billy
Bauer over Russian Bots."

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.500
The episode opens with Scott's character,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.463
Chris, picking up a
ride in central London.

00:02:55.463 --> 00:02:59.440
The real-life location of the
shady Smithereen Corporation?

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.890
It's actually Bloomberg's
HQ. On a Google map drop,

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.760
it takes us to Wallbrook
just outside of Bloomberg

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.810
and the City of London Magistrates' Court.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.750
But it's the close-up of
the rideshare app address

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.130
that's particularly interesting.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.000
We learned that the rider is picking up

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.050
from Skillane Street EC4N.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.030
Skillane was one of the
names of the planets

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.720
in "USS Callister," Skillane 4 and Ranek.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.990
Victoria Skillane is
the name of the killer

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:29.140
in season two's "White Bear."

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.550
Her name also crops up
in the hashtags feed

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:32.960
in "Hated in the Nation"

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.223
as #DeathToVictoriaSkillane.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.860
Also in this frame, we
see Bandersnatch Theatre,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.780
a reference to "Bandersnatch,"

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.530
Raiman's, which is the
name of the antagonist in

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.040
"Men Against Fire," and
Dahl's, after Vanessa Dahl

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.860
in "Hated in the Nation."

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.990
There's also Redfield's Theatre, after

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.900
Cooper Redfield, the
protagonist in "Playtest,"

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.333
Dawson's Burger Bar, who is
the doctor in "Black Museum,"

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.130
and there's two more
interesting place names.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.700
The first is Huq's, a supposed nod to

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.550
Charlie Brooker's wife Konnie Huq,

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.260
who's a TV personality in the UK.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.350
The second is Fence's Pizza.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.930
This takeout joint was also

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.930
featured in "USS
Callister" and "Crocodile."

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.380
We see an empty Fence's Pizza box later on

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.073
in "Smithereens" when
police raid Chris' house.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.670
A quick look at a phone

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.650
also gives us some references to other

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.080
characters in other episodes.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.250
Carlton Bloom, the main antagonist
of "The National Anthem"

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.480
is in there, as is Clayton Leigh,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.816
the abused prisoner in "Black Museum."

00:04:35.816 --> 00:04:38.150
These characters live such disparate lives

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:39.430
in the "Black Mirror" universe

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.620
that it's hard to imagine these

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.420
numbers reference the actual characters.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.130
Chris drives past a BRB
Connect petrol station.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:47.520
Fans might recognize this gas

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.640
station chain from "Black Museum,"

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.230
as well as the acronym
"BRB," "Be Right Back" from

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.300
season two, episode one.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:57.800
An eagle-eyed Reddit user

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.950
also spotted "BRB" appears on the

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.720
login screen in "Metalhead"
where abbreviations

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.363
for all the season four
episode names are shown.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.890
Then we go to the
interiors of Smithereen HQ.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.540
There's a newscast saying
"Prime Minister Callow

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.180
to meet with EU negotiators in Brussels,"

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.050
in reference to Prime Minister Callow from

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.040
season one, episode one.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.910
The hashtag #snoutrage

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.300
also presumably refers
to this episode too.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.550
But take a closer look at

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.595
some of the other hashtags.

00:05:27.595 --> 00:05:30.910
#AshleyOWembley previews Miley Cyrus'

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:32.510
gig in the following episode,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.790
"Rachel, Jack and Ashley Too."

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:39.174
Also hashtagged are
#SeaofTranquillityReboot

00:05:39.174 --> 00:05:42.820
and #SaitoXNetflix, which suggests

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.560
that the company from "Playtest" has

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.397
bought out a new game
or series with Netflix.

00:05:47.397 --> 00:05:50.560
#StJunipersStrike is also on screen,

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.180
which refers to the fictional hospital

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.400
St. Juniper's from "Black Museum."

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.280
Interestingly, this shows that
"Smithereens," "Nosedive,"

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:01.670
"Playtest," "Black Museum,"
and "The National Anthem"

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.640
all take place in the same time

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.570
in the same universe.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.600
Also, because it's Charlie Brooker,

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.750
the seemingly mundane BBC show

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.003
"Bargain Hunt" is also trending.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.650
Any Radiohead fans out there?

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.520
If you weren't content with
"Exit Music for a Film"

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.330
being used in "Shut Up and Dance,"

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:23.973
this episode also features
a 2016 song, "Daydreaming."

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.520
Moving on to episode three,
"Rachel, Jack, and Ashley Too"

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.510
plays with Miley Cyrus' own
experiences as a pop icon

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.480
as she is forced to
repress elements of her

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.720
own personality to become a palatable

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:41.030
bite-sized toy consciousness, Ashley Too.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:42.840
The episode makes two really great

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.180
references to modern pop stardom.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.590
The first is "clone
theory": that pop stars

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:49.470
like Avril Lavigne and Taylor Swift have

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.110
actually been replaced with clones and

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.560
aren't who they say they are.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.360
The second is hologram technology.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:57.950
Artists like Mariah Carey have been

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.850
criticized for embracing hologram

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.830
versions of themselves, which can

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.380
simultaneously perform at different

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.030
venues around the world.

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:06.790
Brooker and Jones seem to be suggesting

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.560
through Ashley's hologram that this

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.090
isn't a true representation of the

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.950
singer and so shouldn't be pursued.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.110
The transplant tech
featured in this episode

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.900
isn't new to "Black Mirror."

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.130
In "White Christmas" and "Black Museum,"

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:21.830
we've seen the perils of
transplanted consciousness

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:25.840
from a person into an inanimate object.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.883
In an interesting time overlap, we see

00:07:27.883 --> 00:07:30.600
that the rapper Tusk from
"Hated in the Nation"

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.113
is making headlines.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.560
On a newscast, we see the
news that, along with a

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.350
Striking Vipers reference,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.320
"Museum owner's body
found in smoking ruins."

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.103
Surely this is a nod to the
events of "Black Museum."

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.440
"Simpsons" fans might
feel a little deja vu

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.220
at the line, "Believe in
yourself. You can do anything."

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.720
The facile line spouted by Ashley Too

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.980
is eerily similar to "Trust in

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.330
yourself, and you can achieve anything"

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.080
that the doll Lisa Lionheart makes

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.123
when Lisa Simpson pulls her cord.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.160
Brooker fans might have
noticed that teddy bears

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.100
crop up across the series,

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.180
as does a sinister
organization, White Bear.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:13.253
This time, it's the name
of a shady security guard.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.090
We finally got a nod to
Charlie Brooker's series

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:18.330
"Nathan Barley."

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.170
The series about an

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.300
east London creative was a satire on

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.730
media life in the mid-2000s.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.890
In it, a character gets
in Sugar Ape magazine.

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.010
The magazine with a different cover star

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.330
is pictured hanging on the wall

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:31.603
of a record company.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:33.660
The series ends with the

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.500
characters exiting Bob's music venue.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:37.827
Bob was the name of the antagonist in

00:08:37.827 --> 00:08:40.650
"USS Callister," hinting that Ashley O has

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.123
embraced her darker side.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.120
Think you spotted anything
that we've missed?

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.287
Comment below.

